Chapter: EffectWidenScopeDeny

Section: Body

 The unsound direction of the scope-drop widening, and the reason it is
 written as a lookup toward an UNSCOPED parameter label rather than as a
 comparison of two scopes.

 `plain-print` may write to any channel. `apply-scoped` is handed it while
 believing it holds something that writes only to stdout -- and passes that
 belief on to its own caller, who granted only `Console "stdout"`. If this
 compiled, a scope would be a suggestion.

 It must stay refused: nothing ever widens TOWARD a scoped label, because
 `label-index-named` matches only parameter labels with no scope.

  plain-print : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  plain-print (t) = act
    print-line-uni t
  end

  apply-scoped : (Text -> [Console "stdout"] Nothing), Text -> [Console "stdout"] Nothing
  apply-scoped (f) (t) = act
    f t
  end

  opening : [Console "stdout"] Nothing = act
    apply-scoped plain-print "laundered"
  end
